# Level files

These are simple ASCII level data files that are read in at run time by the
current rayboing (raylib) build of XBoing.

Use the new.data file as a template for new levels. Consult demo.data to see
the different block characters that can be placed.

## File structure

Each level file has three parts:

    Genesis          <- line 1: level name
    120              <- line 2: time bonus, in seconds
    .........        <- lines 3 to 17: block grid (15 rows of 9 characters)
    .........
    .........        (15 rows in total)

- Line 1: the level name (informational only).
- Line 2: the starting time for the level countdown timer, in seconds.
- Lines 3 to 17: the block grid, read row by row from the top left. Each row
  is exactly 9 characters and there are 15 rows. A dot (.) is an empty cell;
  any other character places a block (see the block index below). Characters
  past column 9 and rows past 15 are ignored.

The shipped levels are named level00.data through level80.data. The game
starts on resource/levels/level01.data.

A blank level looks like this (see new.data):

    New level
    60
    .........
    .........
    ... (15 rows of 9 dots in total)

## Block index

Every cell can hold a dot (empty) or one of the following characters.
Character case matters: r and R are different blocks.

| Character   | Block Name       | Description                                                 |
| ----------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| **H**       | `HYPERSPACE_BLK` | Hyperspace block — no effect yet (not implemented).         |
| **B**       | `BULLET_BLK`     | Bullet block — hitting it increases the ball speed.         |
| **c**       | `MAXAMMO_BLK`    | Maximum ammo block — no effect (not implemented).           |
| **r**       | `RED_BLK`        | Red-colored block.                                          |
| **g**       | `GREEN_BLK`      | Green-colored block.                                        |
| **b**       | `BLUE_BLK`       | Blue-colored block.                                         |
| **t**       | `TAN_BLK`        | Tan-colored block.                                          |
| **p**       | `PURPLE_BLK`     | Purple-colored block.                                       |
| **y**       | `YELLOW_BLK`     | Yellow-colored block.                                       |
| **w**       | `BLACK_BLK`      | Solid wall block — cannot be destroyed and is not counted in the blocks remaining. |
| **0–5**     | `COUNTER_BLK[n]` | Counter block showing a number — each hit lowers the number; it breaks like a normal block when it reaches 0. |
| **+**       | `ROAMER_BLK`     | Roamer block — no effect yet (does not move; not implemented). |
| **X**       | `BOMB_BLK`       | Bomb block — destroys itself and the 8 blocks around it.    |
| **D**       | `DEATH_BLK`      | Death block — no effect yet (does not destroy the ball; not implemented). |
| **L**       | `EXTRABALL_BLK`  | Extra ball block — no effect yet (does not grant a life; not implemented). |
| **M**       | `MGUN_BLK`       | Machine gun block — no effect yet (not implemented).        |
| **W**       | `WALLOFF_BLK`    | Wall-off block — no effect yet (does not toggle walls; not implemented). |
| **?**       | `RANDOM_BLK`     | Random block — no effect yet (behaves like a plain block; not implemented). |
| **d**       | `DROP_BLK`       | Dropping block — no effect yet (does not fall; not implemented). |
| **T**       | `TIMER_BLK`      | Extra time block — no effect yet (does not add time; not implemented). |
| **m**       | `MULTIBALL_BLK`  | Multi-ball block — no effect yet (does not spawn balls; not implemented). |
| **s**       | `STICKY_BLK`     | Sticky block — makes the ball stick to the paddle on the next paddle hit. |
| **R**       | `REVERSE_BLK`    | Reverse control block — toggles reversed paddle movement.   |
| **<**       | `PAD_SHRINK_BLK` | Shrink paddle block — makes the paddle smaller.             |
| **>**       | `PAD_EXPAND_BLK` | Expand paddle block — makes the paddle bigger.              |
| *(default)* | *(none)*         | Any other character — no block is placed, the cell stays empty. |

## Block behavior

When the ball hits a block, `activateBlock()` in `src/demo_blockloader.c`
applies its effect:

- A wall (`w`) is never destroyed and is not counted in the blocks remaining
  counter.
- A bomb (`X`) clears the 3x3 square around itself (walls are left alone).
- Counter blocks (`1`-`5`) drop one number per hit (`5` -> `4` -> ... -> `0`);
  a `0` counter breaks like a normal block.
- `B`, `s`, `R`, `<` and `>` do the effects listed in the table above.
- All other blocks are simply destroyed in one hit.
- The level is won when every block except walls has been destroyed.
- Unknown characters are ignored and create no block.
